Verdict: PASS. I wrote it to session_plan/eval_task_1.md in the required format.

The fix does what #1223 asked. A quoted mention like git commit -m "fix rm -rf / at $(date)" is no longer hard-blocked, and commands that actually run the delete are still refused.

• What changed: before, any $( or backtick sent the whole command to the scan that deletes every quote character. Now that scan only runs when a quote never closes. When the quotes balance, each $(…) or backtick body that bash would run is checked again as a command of its own.
• What I checked myself: I compiled a throwaway test against the committed code in a temporary copy of the repo, then deleted it; nothing in the repo was changed. It confirmed #1223's commit-message row passes and echo "rm -rf /" passes. These all still refuse:
  • bash -c and eval with a substitution in the string
  • echo "$(echo hi; rm -rf /)"
  • rm -rf / date 
  • chains joined with &&, ; and |
  • an unquoted $( rm -rf / )

  The new tests also cover the cases the task said must keep refusing: quote-split words like r"m", single-quoted bodies (treated as plain text), bodies that never close, and unbalanced quotes.
• One case that passes, as before: $(echo rm) -rf / is not blocked. The old code didn't block it either, so the fix didn't introduce it.
• Not reusing an existing helper: it adds its own small quote scanner instead of reusing strip_quoted_regions, as the task preferred. The code comment explains why: that helper would also blank r"m" and quoted arguments inside a substitution body, which must still be read as commands.
• Loose ends: every new function and constant is used in the same diff, and the matching sentence in docs/src/troubleshooting/safety.md was updated. Nothing touches config defaults, flags or startup.

I only judged the diff and the code. I didn't check the issue comments the task required: the before and after tables on #1223 and the old-vs-new comparison.

